# {{ hardening_kernel_conf_path }}
#
# MANAGED BY ANSIBLE — role `hardening_kernel`. Local edits are overwritten on
# the next run. Change the values in that role's defaults/main.yml, or override
# `hardening_kernel_sysctl_extra` in inventory group_vars.
#
# Spec: dev-deployment-SOP.md Step 13.6, hardening-spec.md sec-C5.
#
# Why `99-`, when the hardened sshd drop-in is `00-`: the two directories have
# OPPOSITE precedence. sshd_config.d is first-match-wins, so hardening must sort
# first (SOP Step 13.3). /etc/sysctl.d is last-write-wins, so hardening must
# sort LAST — after /usr/lib/sysctl.d/10-magic-sysrq.conf, 50-coredump.conf and
# anything the cloud image ships. Renaming this file to match the sshd one would
# quietly hand those defaults the win.
#
# DELIBERATELY OMITTED: net.ipv4.ip_forward = 0
#   Most Linux hardening guides recommend it. Do NOT add it here. Docker
#   requires IP forwarding and turns it straight back on, so the only thing the
#   line achieves is a declared value that permanently disagrees with the
#   running kernel — which makes every future audit of this file read as a
#   failure. SOP Step 13.6 and hardening-spec sec-C5 both call this out as the
#   one exception for this deployment. If you are here to "fix" it: it is not
#   broken.
#
# kernel.core_pattern is the one entry that is in sec-C5 but not in the SOP's
# Step 13.6 block. Reason: hardening-spec sec-B7 — every Buzz secret is in the
# relay's process environment, and a core dump writes that environment to disk.
# It is paired with fs.suid_dumpable = 0 below.

{% for key, value in hardening_kernel_sysctl_effective.items() %}
{{ key }} = {{ value }}
{% endfor %}
